# LEVEL 14 → 15 — SENDING DATA TO A TCP PORT WITH NETCAT


## LEVEL GOAL
Submit the current level's password to the service running on `localhost:30000` to retrieve the next password.

## DESCRIPTION
This level involves direct TCP communication rather than file reading, introducing `nc` (Netcat) — a foundational tool in networking and security.

## APPROACH
I initially attempted to SSH into port 30000, which failed — port 30000 is not an SSH service:

```bash
# This fails — port 30000 is not an SSH service
ssh bandit15@localhost -p 30000
```

The correct approach was Netcat:

```bash
nc localhost 30000
```

After the connection opened, I pasted the current level's password and pressed Enter. The server responded with the next password.

## SSH vs. Netcat

Tool        Use Case 

`ssh`       Encrypted remote login with session management 
`nc`        Raw TCP/UDP connection — no protocol overhead 
`telnet`    Legacy unencrypted TCP communication 

> Password obtained. [Not shared per OverTheWire rules]

## KEY TAKEAWAY
Not all network services are SSH. Netcat is called the "Swiss Army knife of networking" because it can interact with any TCP or UDP service directly. It is used extensively in penetration testing, CTF challenges, and network diagnostics.
